WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.620
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners Episode 83 for June 25th, 2026.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:10.600
Some awesome AI developments today, and we're going to make all of it make sense.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:11.860
Let's get into it.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:12.680
Picture this.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:17.780
You tell your phone assistant to book a table, but instead of just replying with a link,

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:23.220
it actually opens the app, taps the right buttons, and finishes the whole thing for you.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:29.200
That is exactly the kind of leap Google just made with its Gemini 3.5 Flash model.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:35.880
They added a feature called computer use, which gives the AI the ability to look at your screen and control the mouse.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.240
Click buttons and type on its own.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:46.460
Think of it like handing your usual voice assistant a pair of virtual hands so it can finish tasks instead of only describing what to do.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.200
The system works by taking a picture of everything on your screen,

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.760
breaking the image into recognizable pieces like buttons and text boxes,

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.300
then deciding the next useful action.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:03.140
On a test called OS World that measures how well AI handles real computer tasks,

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:08.660
it scored 78.4, putting it on the same level as other leading models.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:18.280
For students, this could eventually mean an AI that organizes files or runs practice quizzes on actual websites without step-by-step instructions from you.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:26.440
For anyone thinking about future careers, it shows AI shifting from answering questions to working alongside people on longer projects.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:34.220
Developers can already experiment with the Gemini API to build simple agents that test apps or handle repetitive clicks.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:40.760
If you are not a coder, the easiest way to see the direction is to open Gemini on your phone or computer,

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:45.840
and notice how it already understands images and contacts better than older versions.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:52.640
Over time, this turns everyday tools into something closer to having a patient helper who can simply do the thing.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.400
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the Deep Dive where we look under the hood.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:02.200
You know how your phone guesses the next word while you are texting?

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:07.260
Now, stretch that same idea to guessing the next action on a screen instead.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:11.940
First, the AI snaps a picture of everything open on your computer.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:19.220
Then it breaks that picture into small, understandable pieces, the same way you spot a door or a light switch in a room.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:25.480
Next, it predicts the most useful next move, such as clicking a search bar or typing an address,

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.260
just like auto-complete predicts a full sentence from a few letters.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.340
Finally, it sends the chosen action back to the computer so the cursor actually moves and clicks.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.680
And that is basically what computer use AI is doing when it operates your screen.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.680
So next time someone says an AI can control your device,

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:50.720
you can explain it is really just super-powered screen auto-complete trained on millions of examples.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:53.420
Not so scary once you see the pattern, right?

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.860
Let's check out some other cool stuff you can try right now.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:04.020
Open AI just released a new version of GPT 5.5 instant that feels more natural when you chat with it.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:11.360
It handles messy questions better and gives more reliable answers for things like shopping ideas or local suggestions.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:17.440
Paid users can try it immediately by opening chat GPT and starting a new chat,

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:19.940
while free users will see it tomorrow.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:25.640
Here is a specific test. Ask it to recommend three movies based on a show you like,

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:29.200
then add the extra rule that nothing should have subtitles,

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:32.040
and watch how it adjusts its suggestions.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:36.160
The second tool comes from Figma, the design platform.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:42.600
They added AI features that create motion graphics and shiny lighting effects just from a text description.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:48.800
Instead of drawing every frame yourself, you describe the movement and the AI fills it in.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:53.660
This works great for TikTok videos, school presentations, or game art,

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:58.160
go to Figma.com, make a free account, open a new design file,

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.140
and search for the new AI motion tools.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:05.220
Type something like, make this circle bounce like a basketball,

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:06.900
and see the animation appear.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:09.440
A couple quick bits before we wrap.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:14.600
Google now keeps images and searches you upload so its models can learn from them.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:19.760
Many people send photos and queries to Google services on a regular basis.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:24.460
The company incorporates this material into training for its AI systems.

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:29.520
Users who prefer to prevent that use have a direct way to adjust the behavior.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:35.740
The controls sit inside your Google account in the section called search history and data controls.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:38.520
Making the change takes less than a minute.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:42.380
After the setting is updated, your activity stays more private.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:48.520
Separately, a recent report showed that chat bots like Chad GPT give answers matching

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:52.620
left-leaning views about 80% of the time on political topics.

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:57.700
Researchers tested several models and saw the same pattern across different questions.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:01.940
The pattern appears consistently no matter which political issue is presented.

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:07.040
The result simply reflects the internet text the systems learned from during training.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:12.560
The finding serves as a reminder that model outputs mere the data they were built on.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:17.200
No single company has announced changes to address the observed tendency yet.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:18.440
And that's a wrap.

